WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.413 --> 00:00:09.100
In this video, I'll say a little about
using the course evaluation rubric.

2
00:00:11.470 --> 00:00:16.910
The most common use of an evaluation
rubric is to achieve uniformity in grades

3
00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:19.994
when a team of experts is grading
a large collection of examinations.

4
00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:24.707
The goal is to assign a reliable
grade to each candidate's work.

5
00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:27.350
In particular,

6
00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:31.210
it's assumed that the people doing
the evaluation are experienced graders.

7
00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:35.940
In this class, we're using
a rubric in a very different way.

8
00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:40.874
Not to help experts produce
a reliable grade of a piece of work,

9
00:00:40.874 --> 00:00:45.740
but as a mechanism to help
beginners learn about proofs.

10
00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:50.740
If you look at the rubric for the course,

11
00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:54.176
you see that it asks you to consider
five different features of proofs.

12
00:00:54.176 --> 00:00:57.547
Logical correctness, clarity,

13
00:00:57.547 --> 00:01:02.320
opening, stating the conclusion and
reasons.

14
00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:05.390
I'll come to the sixth category later,
by the way.

15
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.480
These are all important
features of proofs.

16
00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:11.720
And for pedagogic reasons,
are given equal weight.

17
00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:13.350
Here's why.

18
00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:17.370
As I state repeatedly
throughout the course,

19
00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:20.710
proofs do much more than simply
establish that some statement is true.

20
00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:26.730
Actually, in practice they don't really
do that, they simply provide evidence

21
00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:30.990
that convinces a reasonable person
with sufficient background knowledge.

22
00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:32.220
But let's leave that issue for now.

23
00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:37.210
If you look at the role played
by proofs in mathematics,

24
00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:40.510
you discover that just as
important as logical correctness

25
00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:43.110
is the understandability of
the proof by an intended reader.

26
00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:48.250
In fact, in practical terms
understandability is more important than

27
00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:49.270
logical correctness.

28
00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:54.010
There's probably no modern
proof of more than a few pages

29
00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:56.850
that does not initially have
a logical flow buried somewhere.

30
00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:02.610
What makes proofs useful is that
others can not only detect errors, but

31
00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:05.090
also have enough information
to try to correct them.

32
00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:10.100
Proofs are as much about
communication as establishing truth.

33
00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:16.350
Because we have to use simple examples
to develop proof evaluation skills,

34
00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:18.400
in most of the examples you see, one or

35
00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:23.270
more of the rubric criteria will apply
only in a simplistic way if at all.

36
00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:29.875
But in more complicated examples
they will all be important.

37
00:02:29.875 --> 00:02:32.367
That's why the five criteria
carry equal weight.

38
00:02:32.367 --> 00:02:37.541
When you evaluate arguments,
you should view them in terms of

39
00:02:37.541 --> 00:02:42.626
each of the five rubric criteria
in turn on an equal footing.

40
00:02:42.626 --> 00:02:45.345
True, experts rarely use rubrics.

41
00:02:45.345 --> 00:02:49.177
They're so familiar with the proof
concept that they automatically and

42
00:02:49.177 --> 00:02:52.209
holistically judge in terms
of all those perspectives.

43
00:02:55.586 --> 00:03:00.186
For a beginner, the rubric breaks up
the task of judging the overall quality of

44
00:03:00.186 --> 00:03:02.920
a proof to judging those
five aspects in turn.

45
00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:11.164
Each of those five is considerably
simpler to do than the entire task,

46
00:03:11.164 --> 00:03:14.167
but you'll still find it difficult.

47
00:03:14.167 --> 00:03:18.665
After you've judged a proof in terms of
the five proof characteristics, the rubric

48
00:03:18.665 --> 00:03:22.674
asks you to bring them all together to
produce a single grade for the proof.

49
00:03:25.922 --> 00:03:29.570
This is the number you would enter
if your task were to assign a single

50
00:03:29.570 --> 00:03:31.695
numerical grade to someone's proof.

51
00:03:34.238 --> 00:03:39.415
It corresponds to the number an expert
working without a rubric would assign.

52
00:03:39.415 --> 00:03:43.865
The rubric is designed to assist you as
a beginner to arrive at that final grade.

53
00:03:47.732 --> 00:03:51.152
Incidentally, when you're evaluating
the work of another student,

54
00:03:51.152 --> 00:03:53.840
as you would in test flight if you do it.

55
00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:56.900
You do owe it to that student to be
as fair and accurate as possible.

56
00:03:58.738 --> 00:04:02.210
But no one expects accuracy from someone
who is just beginning to learn about

57
00:04:02.210 --> 00:04:02.730
proofs.

58
00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:07.870
Because everyone's work is
evaluated by three fellow students,

59
00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:12.760
all anonymously by the way, with
a reported grade computed from those three

60
00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:16.440
grades, plus the individual's
own self grade.

61
00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:21.120
It turns out that most of the time,
the final grade is pretty good.

62
00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:25.650
Nevertheless, the real purpose of
the test flight evaluation process

63
00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:29.530
is that it offers tremendous
benefits to the evaluator

64
00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:32.180
in coming to a better appreciation
of what makes a good proof.

65
00:04:33.890 --> 00:04:38.810
In simple terms, in this course,
the grading is primarily for

66
00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:40.050
the benefit of the grader.